# CocoaPods — the lockfile IS committed (below); the installed pods are not.
ios/Pods/

# The workspace, on the other hand, IS committed — but only the one file inside
# it that `pod install` derives from the Podfile.
#
# Why commit a generated artifact at all: Xcode Cloud picks the project or
# workspace to build by listing what is in the CLONE at workflow-creation time.
# An ignored workspace is not offerable, so the RN app could not be selected as
# a build target at all — the single largest gap in the Xcode Cloud transition
# (docs/planning/research/2026-08-06-xcode-cloud-transition.md §레포 준비 ②).
#
# Why THIS file is safe to commit: `contents.xcworkspacedata` is eleven lines of
# XML holding two relative `group:` FileRefs (the app project, Pods.xcodeproj).
# No absolute path, no UUID, no timestamp — byte-identical across machines,
# worktrees and CocoaPods versions, unlike `Podfile.lock`, whose checksums are
# not (see the hermes-engine note in ios/Podfile). It changes only when a target
# is added to the Podfile, which is a review-worthy event rather than noise.
#
# Why the rest of the bundle stays ignored: `xcuserdata/` (line 14) and
# `xcshareddata/` are IDE state — schemes of the moment, IDEWorkspaceChecks,
# WorkspaceSettings. Neither exists in a freshly generated workspace; they appear
# the first time someone opens it in Xcode, which is exactly when they must not
# start being committed.
ios/*.xcworkspace/*
!ios/*.xcworkspace/contents.xcworkspacedata

